t year and again in 2005. The program, he writes, has certified more than 1,000 Nepali guides who now work on Everest and \"throughout the world.\"\nEven with improved competence and greater authority, he stresses that Everest remains \"exceedingly hazardous\" for Sherpas. Because clients use more supplemental oxygen than before, workers must make more trips through the Khumbu Icefall to ferry canisters, increasing their exposure in what he describes as a constantly shifting, 2,000-foot-high jumble of house-size ice blocks. He adds that rapid warming in the Himalayas is destabilizing the snow-and-ice \"carapace\" across much of the Everest massif, increasing the chance of another mass-casualty event like the avalanche that killed 16 Nepali workers on April 14, 2014.\n\n### Conflict, respect, and a turning point beyond Everest: K2 in winter\nKrakauer argues that foreign climbers have often failed to treat Nepali workers with equal respect, and he recounts how resentment built for decades before erupting in 2013 on an ice face at about 23,000 feet. He relays an account (via Melissa Arnot Reid’s memoir \"Enough\") in which Sherpas asked other climbers to stay away while a large Sherpa team fixed ropes on the Lhotse Face; two European alpinists, Ueli Steck and Simone Moro, and their cinematographer Jonathan Griffith climbed anyway. While staying more than 100 feet away for much of the climb, they eventually had to pass directly above the Sherpas to reach Camp 3, and inadvertently knocked loose ice that struck some Sherpas; he describes the Sherpas as furious both about the hazard and the perceived disrespect of ignoring the closure.\nHe continues with a sequence of escalation: an altercation, an obscene insult directed at Mingma Tenzing (leader of the Sherpa rope team), the rope team abandoning work and descending, and then a confrontation at Camp 2 where \"a mob of 100 angry Sherpas\" attacked, throwing rocks and kicking the Europeans after they fell. Arnot Reid, in this telling, defused the situation by persuading Moro to kneel and apologize, after which the mob dispersed and Steck and Moro retreated down the icefall with minor injuries. Krakauer portrays the episode as ugly but consequential, prompting a more candid reckoning with how Sherpas have taken disproportionate risk since 1921 while rarely being treated as equal partners, a reassessment reinforced by a labor strike after the 2014 avalanche.\nHe then points to a landmark moment of global recognition that did not happen on Everest: on January 16, 2021, a 10-person all-Nepali team completed the first winter ascent of K2 in gale-force winds and -58 degrees temperatures, reaching 28,251 feet and singing Nepal’s national anthem together on the summit. Krakauer emphasizes K2’s reputation as a harder and more dangerous peak than Everest and notes that many strong teams had tried and failed at a winter ascent before this success; he quotes team leader Mingma Gyalje Sherpa describing the achievement as \"about giving justice to our future generations,\" and casts the moment as hard-won respect roughly a century after the first Sherpa deaths on Everest.\n\n*“it’s hard not to look at those pictures of clients stacked on the side of the mountain in long lines ... and not think: Gross.”*\n\n### Environmental damage and the sense that something has been \"sold off\"\nNot all changes are positive in Krakauer’s view. He acknowledges that the springtime influx of guided climbers boosts the regional economy, but he argues it has also been \"highly damaging to the environment,\" and says newer rules for trash and human-waste removal have not adequately addressed the degradation. He treats this as one dimension of harm created by mass participation and commercialization.\nHe also describes a more subjective, experiential loss: even though summiting still takes weeks of hard effort and remains risky, the mountain’s commodification has stripped away much of the profundity that once made Everest feel like a singular encounter with exploration. He leans on journalist Carl Hoffman’s reflection that guiding companies admirably make the climb possible for almost anyone, yet the spectacle of clients \"stacked\" in lines suggests that what is being purchased has drifted so far from the idea of adventure that it can feel \"meaningless.\" Krakauer agrees with that sadness, while still leaving room for a different kind of ascent.\n\n### The old-style alternatives are still there, and they are still brutal\nKrakauer closes by arguing that it remains possible, for those capable and willing, to climb in a more minimal, traditional style, invoking Reinhold Messner’s solo ascent on August 20, 1980. He describes Messner reaching the summit alone in stormy monsoon conditions on the Tibetan side via a partially new route, without bottled oxygen, established camps, fixed rope, or help from anyone, calling it \"the greatest mountaineering feat of all time.\" Short of that extreme, he suggests that climbers who want to avoid the hordes can attempt the colder, snowier autumn season, or avoid the two primary guided routes by taking the direct North Face or the remote Kangshung Face.\nThose options, he adds, come with a blunt tradeoff: you are unlikely to see other people and you will get the adversity you want, but you also \"stand a better chance of getting killed.\" He ends with a note of tempered sympathy: after what he experienced in 1996, he says he is not inclined to fault those who now choose the highest-probability, lowest-effort route to the top.","completed_at":1777974497,"duration_ms":46332,"time_to_first_token_ms":9307}}